# A call site reads its argument's set and picks the rendering path from it.
# Handed over, `print` has no set to read, so its wrapper carries the choice
# into the run: a record reaches the user's arm, everything else renders
# directly. Both halves are here because a wrapper that always dispatched
# would fail on the int, and one that never did would ignore the arm.
type point
  x
  y

fn render/to_string p:point
  "({p.x},{p.y})"

# A none is named at every hop it takes. `shown none` and `apply print none`
# each need an arm that says `none`, because the rule asks the group that is
# handed one to have a name for it and neither of these is the end of the
# journey. The `f none` inside is not a hop the check reads: `f` is a
# parameter, not a group, so there is no arm list to look up.
fn apply f none
  f none

fn apply f x
  f x

fn shown none
  apply print none

fn shown x
  apply print x

pub play =
  shown 7
  >> shown 1.5
  >> shown true
  >> shown none
  >> shown [7 8]
  >> shown "ab"
  >> shown (point 1 2)
